{
#>> Claude Code's own UI, in this machine's two accents. Replaces the old
#>> yellow-border.json (which set only promptBorder + clawd_body) and
#>> monokai.json, every key of which was fiction -- see below.
#>>
#>> THE KEY NAMES HERE ARE NOT NEGOTIABLE AND NOT GUESSABLE. Claude Code merges a
#>> custom theme with `if (Object.hasOwn(base, key) && validColour(value))`, so a
#>> key it does not already have is dropped **silently** -- no error, no warning,
#>> no hint that the file did nothing. That is how monokai.json.in came to carry
#>> 50 VS Code-flavoured names (ui.border, terminal.brightBlue, button.hover,
#>> lineNumberActive) that never existed and never applied. If a key stops working
#>> after a Claude Code update, it was renamed upstream and the failure will look
#>> exactly like this file being ignored. The list can be recovered from the
#>> binary (76 keys as of 2.1.228):
#>>   strings ~/.local/share/claude/versions/<v> \
#>>     | grep -o "autoAccept:.\{0,3000\}" | grep 'text:"rgb(2' | head -1
#>>
#>> Accepted value forms are rgb(r,g,b), #rrggbb, #rgb, ansi256(n) and
#>> ansi:<name>. Plain hex is what the accents already are, so they substitute
#>> straight in with no conversion.
#>>
#>> @CLAUDE_PRIMARY@ / @CLAUDE_SECONDARY@ and NOT @PRIMARY@ / @SECONDARY@: this is
#>> the one file that honours CLAUDE_SWAP, the answer that turns Claude Code's two
#>> accents round without touching the prompt or either status bar. lib/derive.sh
#>> resolves the pair, and the shimmer and tint ramps below come out of the same
#>> answer, so nothing here can end up half swapped.
  "name": "@MACHINE_LOWER@ accents",
  "base": "dark",
  "overrides": {
#>> PRIMARY -- everything that is Claude addressing you. These keys are the light
#>> blue (#b1b9f9 by default) that shows up in permission prompts, the
#>> autocomplete menu and the #-memory hints; the spinner blue (#93a5ff) and the
#>> brand orange (#d77757) join them so the whole conversation reads as one hue.
#>>
#>> "permission" is doing more work than its name suggests and is worth knowing
#>> about before repointing it: the markdown renderer draws every inline `code`
#>> span in Claude's answers with it --
#>>   case"codespan": return ko("permission", t)(e.text)
#>> -- so this key is the highlighted text in the replies as well as the prompt
#>> menus, and there is no separate key to split the two. Nothing else in an
#>> answer is themeable at all: strong is bold, em is italic, headings are bold
#>> and none of the three takes a colour. Worth remembering that a primary of
#>> #999999 is upstream inactive exactly (rgb(153,153,153)), which is how an
#>> accent that IS being applied to codespans can read as unstyled dim text.
    "permission": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "permissionShimmer": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY_SHIMMER@",
    "suggestion": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "remember": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "promptBorder": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "promptBorderShimmer": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY_SHIMMER@",
    "claude": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "claudeShimmer": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY_SHIMMER@",
    "claudeBlue_FOR_SYSTEM_SPINNER": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "claudeBlueShimmer_FOR_SYSTEM_SPINNER": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY_SHIMMER@",
    "clawd_body": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
#>> rate_limit_fill is the filled half of the usage bar and is upstream the same
#>> #b1b9f9 as permission; briefLabelClaude is the "Claude" label in brief mode,
#>> upstream the same brand orange as "claude". Both joined the theme after this
#>> file was first written, which is the ordinary way a key gets missed here --
#>> the merge is silent in that direction too, so an unclaimed key just keeps
#>> upstream colour and looks like a decision.
    "rate_limit_fill": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
    "briefLabelClaude": "@CLAUDE_PRIMARY@",
#>> SECONDARY -- everything that says a MODE is engaged, which is exactly the set
#>> you want to catch your eye against the primary you are reading all day.
#>> Upstream spends four separate hues on these (purple #af87ff, teal #48968c,
#>> pink #fd5db1, blue #4782c8); they are one colour here because what matters is
#>> "a mode is on", and which mode is already written next to the indicator.
#>> fastMode (upstream orange) and effortUltra (upstream the autoAccept purple)
#>> are the same kind of statement and join them.
    "autoAccept": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "autoAcceptShimmer": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY_SHIMMER@",
    "skill": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "merged": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "planMode": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "bashBorder": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "ide": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "fastMode": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
    "fastModeShimmer": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY_SHIMMER@",
    "effortUltra": "@CLAUDE_SECONDARY@",
#>> THE FILLED BLOCKS -- the six keys that are a background under white text
#>> rather than text themselves: your own message, that message hovered, a #
#>> memory, a selection, the empty half of the usage bar, and a bash message.
#>> They are the accent hue at fixed low lightness (the lightnesses upstream
#>> chose for these same keys) with saturation capped at 0.40, NOT positions on
#>> the ramp -- see the "tint" paragraph in lib/derive.sh, which is also where
#>> the reason for the cap is. A grey accent reproduces upstream greys almost
#>> exactly, which is the property that makes the cap the right call.
#>>
#>> bashMessageBackgroundColor takes the SECONDARY tint on purpose: bashBorder is
#>> already the secondary, so the block and the border around it agree.
    "userMessageBackground": "@CLAUDE_MSG_BG@",
    "userMessageBackgroundHover": "@CLAUDE_MSG_BG_HOVER@",
    "memoryBackgroundColor": "@CLAUDE_MEMORY_BG@",
    "selectionBg": "@CLAUDE_SELECTION_BG@",
    "rate_limit_empty": "@CLAUDE_TRACK_BG@",
    "bashMessageBackgroundColor": "@CLAUDE_BASH_BG@"
#>> DELIBERATELY NOT ACCENTED, and the reason is the one slurm-status.sh.in gives
#>> for drawing job state in fixed neon green and yellow: a colour that answers a
#>> question has to answer it the same way on every machine, whatever accents are
#>> set. So error, warning, success, the six diff colours, every
#>> *_FOR_SUBAGENTS_ONLY (they only work by being distinct from each other), the
#>> seven rainbow_* pairs (same), briefLabelYou (it only works by differing from
#>> briefLabelClaude above), and text / inverseText / inactive / subtle /
#>> background all keep upstream values.
  }
}
